## 5. Self Projects (0-25 points)

**Complexity (one table, stated once):** simple/low-impact projects are todo
apps, calculators, basic CRUD apps, weather apps built on a public API,
note-taking apps, recipe apps, simple portfolio sites, "Hello World"/classroom
exercises, basic sentiment analysis with standard libraries (NLTK,
scikit-learn), and simple e-commerce or social-media clones. Complex/high-impact
projects are multi-feature full-stack apps, apps with real user authentication
*and* a real database, ML/AI applications, real-time apps (chat, streaming),
mobile apps with native features, microservices architecture, apps with genuine
user adoption, apps solving a real-world problem, or apps demonstrating advanced
algorithms/data structures.

**Basic CRUD apps — one rule:** score 0-3. Zero is reserved for a CRUD app with
no distinguishing feature at all; 1-3 requires a genuine added feature (real
auth, a non-trivial data model, etc). There is no separate "give zero points"
rule on top of this — this is the only CRUD rule.

**Bands (before link adjustment):** 18-25 for complex projects with real-world
impact, advanced architecture, multiple technologies, or genuine user adoption;
9-17 for projects with some complexity, good documentation, or a moderate
technical challenge; 1-8 for simple/tutorial projects (the 0-3 CRUD rule sits
inside this band, not on top of it); 0 for no projects, or projects
demonstrating no technical skill at all.

**Link adjustment — the only mechanism that affects this score for link status:**
after assigning a band above, adjust down one sub-tier (upper half of the band
to the lower half of the same band, or the bottom of a band to the top of the
next band down) if a project has no verifiable link — no GitHub, live demo, or
active URL. Adjust down half a sub-tier if the link is broken or inactive. No
separate percentage reduction or flat-point deduction applies anywhere else for
link status.

**Track carve-out (stated once):** for `formal_it`/`related_field` candidates
who are current full-time students, a portfolio consisting mainly of coursework
projects is expected and must not, by itself, push the score below what the
rules above would otherwise produce. This carve-out does not apply to
`self_taught` candidates, for whom project history is a primary signal.

